\subsection{What We Found}

\paragraph{The ordering survives, and TabICL still leads.} Mean
$\mathit{rel}$ over all @@nAll@@ sets is @@all_b1@@ for a single tree,
@@mu_lgbm@@ for LightGBM and @@all_b30@@ for {\tt enuf(30)}; TabICL, on
the @@nRan@@ sets under its row cap, is @@mu_tab@@. The ranked-best
counts in Table~\ref{tab:regfolds} say it by counting, over those same
@@nRan@@ sets: TabICL first on @@w_tab@@, LightGBM on @@w_lgbm@@,
{\tt enuf(30)} on @@w_b30@@, the single tree on @@w_b1@@.

So one of \S\ref{sec:tabicl5x2}'s two claims travels and one does not.
TabICL leading every group under a different loss on a different corpus
is the stronger result, and we did not expect it to be this clean. But
the tie we reported between {\tt enuf(30)} and LightGBM does not
survive: in classification {\tt enuf(30)} was ranked best on 14 sets
against LightGBM's 9, and here the count runs the other way,
@@w_lgbm@@ against @@w_b30@@, with LightGBM ahead on mean
$\mathit{rel}$ as well. We report that rather than bury it. The next
paragraphs are about where the reversal comes from, and the short answer
is that it is about size, and about a cap in our own configuration.

