\paragraph{The ensemble pays, once it is fed.} An earlier draft of
this section reported a puzzle: the one-to-thirty step barely moved
the needle, and on the {\tt config} sets the single tree {\em won}
outright. Bagging is supposed to be a variance killer; ours was a
rounding error that sometimes ran backwards. Two suspects, both ours
rather than the method's: {\tt the.cap} trimmed training to @@cap@@
rows before any tree was built (each tree then saw half of those, so
thirty @@caphalf@@-row trees were asked to beat one @@cap@@-row tree
--- a trade that pays on AUC and fails here); and regression sampled
$|x|/3$ columns per cut, which on configuration data --- where the
signal concentrates in a few options --- mostly sampled noise.

Both were one-line fixes, and cheap iteration made the test trivial:
a scratch harness crossed cap, column sampling, leaf size and fold
policy over a random 18 sets in minutes. Two popular suspects were
acquitted on the spot --- leaf size 20 was catastrophic (+45\% error:
the deep leaves are load-bearing) and 5x5 folds changed nothing (the
cross-validation policy was never the artifact). Feeding regression
@@rcap@@ rows and every column was the whole story. Mean
$\mathit{rel}$ fell from 0.57 to @@all_b30@@ over all @@nAll@@ sets and from 0.43 to
@@cfg_b30@@ on {\tt config}, where the inversion is gone
(@@cfg_b1@@ for one tree against @@cfg_b30@@ for thirty); over all
@@nAll@@ sets the thirty-tree step now cuts @@gain@@\% off a single
tree and wins on @@beat@@.

One suspect stays open for the next draft: a {\tt enuf} leaf
predicts its {\em mean} and {\tt predict} {\em averages} across
trees; both steps minimise {\em squared} error, yet we score {\em
absolute} error, whose minimiser is the median. Re-scoring on RMSE
--- or predicting medians --- costs little and is not yet run, and
\S\ref{sec:prompts} is about saying which is which.


